 {"id":"fabro-2e3c","title":"engine: auto_approve not effective through the git-source intent path (children still wait approval_required)","status":"closed","type":"bug","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-05T17:21:38.401Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-05T21:38:44.449Z","description":"Conductor pass 01M1S6MSV4 passed auto_approve:true on fabro_run_create (visible in the tool-call args); git_source::intent_from_spec maps it into RunIntentArgs.auto_approve, but BOTH children (develop 01M1S6VRWQ, revisor 01M1S9C1H8) still landed in pending/approval_required and needed manual approval (2026-09-05). The intent admission path apparently does not apply args.auto_approve as the pre-execution approval (the legacy manifest path filtered/honored it). Fix: create_run_from_intent admission must honor RunIntentArgs.auto_approve (set approval to Auto or record the approval event at admission). Blocks fully autonomous conductor children. Basis: runs 01M1S6VRWQMD56M1X8HWAXDSNN + 01M1S9C1H8XVVEEB62JQKPXKY3, commits 5f413bbc7/7fda51235.","labels":["engine","workflows"],"closedAt":"2026-09-05T21:38:44.449Z","closeReason":"Fixed and production-proven (commit aa5c598a9): the start handler's parent-worker approval gate now honors the resolved approval mode — args.auto_approve=true (-> ApprovalMode::Auto) starts orchestration children directly; every other parent-worker spawn keeps the anti-runaway guard. Proof: conductor pass 01M1SQYDJMHSVJYMZB3C643K7J created merge-upstream child 01M1SR27KEJGNQ which went straight to running with NO pending/approval_required. 920/920 fabro-server tests green incl. new companion tests (auto_approve starts runnable; without the flag the gate still parks)."}
 {"id":"fabro-cfd6","title":"ci: prebuilt cargo-chef gate image on GHCR — cut dogfood-gate Rust time from 10-15min to ~5min","status":"open","type":"feature","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-05T22:46:54.814Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-07T13:23:41.582Z","description":"GitHub-hosted runners start empty: rust-cache covers deps only on cache hits (misses/evictions recompile everything) and never covers toolchain+nextest setup or the workspace itself, so dogfood-gate Rust runs take 10-15 min — and the conductor waits for that merge (direct line latency, observed PRs #28/#30). Wanted: cargo-chef recipe layer baked into a gate image pushed to GHCR; dogfood-gate runs in that container so dependency compilation and toolchain come from image layers. Rebuild the image post-merge on denkhaus (incremental via GHCR build cache) plus nightly for Cargo.lock drift. Expected: ~4-7 min per code PR. Basis: PRs #28/#30 gate timings, conductor passes 01M1S6MSV4/01M1SQYDJM.","labels":["ci","workflows"],"assignee":"fabro"}
 {"id":"fabro-dcd4","title":"web: automation pause button is a no-op stub — wire pause/resume to replaceAutomation","status":"closed","type":"bug","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-06T08:25:11.350Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-06T09:45:36.782Z","description":"The Pause-schedule button on automation cards (apps/fabro-web/app/routes/automations.tsx, AutomationCard ~lines 169-176) has NO onClick handler: clicking does nothing, no request is sent, no state changes. The card is also blind to the schedule trigger's enabled flag: AutomationRow.schedule only carries the expression (findScheduleTrigger(a)?.expression), so the list shows the Pause button even when the schedule is already disabled and offers no resume path. Fix: extend AutomationRow with scheduleEnabled (findScheduleTrigger(a)?.enabled === true) and keep the raw Automation for the request body; make the button a toggle that calls automationsApi.replaceAutomation(id, revision, body) with the schedule trigger's enabled flipped (pattern: automations-edit.tsx:107 — If-Match via automation.revision, then mutate(queryKeys.automations.list()), toast on success/error, busy spinner like the Run button); Pause icon + 'Pause schedule' when enabled, Play icon + 'Resume schedule' when disabled; mark the paused schedule badge (amber 'paused') so the state is visible. Tests: pause sends PUT with schedule enabled:false, resume sends enabled:true, If-Match equals current revision. Observed live 2026-09-06: user clicked Pause for the conductor automation in the web UI, nothing happened; the schedule had been paused out-of-band via API (revision 32ea7dfb).","labels":["web","ux"],"closedAt":"2026-09-06T09:45:36.782Z","closeReason":"Landed: AutomationCard pause/resume toggle wired to automationsApi.replaceAutomation (If-Match revision, schedule trigger enabled flipped), amber paused badge, busy spinner, toast on success/error; AutomationRow carries scheduleEnabled + raw; tests: pause PUT enabled:false, resume PUT enabled:true, If-Match revision, paused badge (3 tests, full web suite 719/719, typecheck clean)."}
-{"id":"fabro-4814","title":"Develop prompts: purge retired two-worlds framing, make project-agnostic for cross-project reuse","status":"open","type":"task","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-06T08:59:49.942Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-07T13:23:41.715Z","description":"URGENT (user directive 2026-09-06). The develop workflow prompts still carry the retired two-worlds model that ADR-0013 (world merger d9138572c) removed: implementer.md says 'You build the PRODUCT — on this world that is the Rust workspace under lib/' and builds a whole 'Platform scope is off-limits' section on the product/platform split; planner.md and reviewer.md repeat platform/product carve-out language, fs_hide world bindings, and repo-specific path lists (.fabro/, .seeds/, .mulch/, .agents/, scripts/, justfile). Goal: ALL develop workflow prompts (planner.md, implementer.md, reviewer.md, plus workflow.fabro/workflow.toml attributes and scripts/*.nu that carry world language) reformulated GENERALLY and PROJECT-AGNOSTICALLY, so the workflow is reusable in another project with minimal effort (user directive: 'möglichst generell und projekt-agnostisch, sodass ein workflow mit nur geringen aufwand in anderen Projekten wiederverwendet werden kann'). Approach directions: (1) replace world-scoped scope rules with a single capability-based scope description driven by the per-node fs/tool envelope (ADR-0009 family), not product/platform prose; (2) externalize every repo-specific fact (path lists, tracker commands, gate commands, pinned toolchains) into workflow settings/context values or one PROJECT_FACTS block so prompts stay generic; (3) keep the stage contract (journal keys, PASS/FAIL reporting, seed workflow) unchanged — this is a framing/portability rework, not a behavior change; (4) sweep for 'world', 'platform', 'product', 'denkhaus', 'lab' phrasing and rewrite each hit. Verification: grep shows zero two-worlds phrasing; a develop qualification cycle still passes end to end. Basis: .fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/{planner,implementer,reviewer}.md, ADR-0013 (repo docs/lab/adr/0013*), ADR-0009 stage-envelope family.","labels":["workflows","revision"],"assignee":"fabro"}
